addEN("a1-1",{"body": "\n<h2>1. What is an algorithm?</h2>\n<p><b>An algorithm</b> is a finite, precisely defined sequence of steps that turns an input into a desired output. To count as an \"algorithm\" a method needs five properties: <b>input</b>, <b>output</b>, <b>definiteness</b> (every step unambiguous), <b>finiteness</b> (it ends after a finite number of steps) and <b>effectiveness</b> (every step can be carried out with real resources).</p>\n<p>Two different questions must be asked about any algorithm: (1) <b>Is it correct?</b> Does it give the right output for every valid input? (2) <b>Is it efficient?</b> How much time and memory does it need, and how does that grow as the input grows? This lesson is about the second question. Correctness is usually proved by induction or by a loop invariant.</p>\n<h2>2. Why \"analysis\" and not just timing?</h2>\n<p>Measured running time depends on the hardware, the language, the compiler and the test inputs. In theoretical analysis we use the <b>RAM model</b>: every basic operation (addition, comparison, reading/writing one memory cell) has unit cost, and we ask how the number of these operations depends on the <b>input size</b> $n$. We call that function $T(n)$. The goal is to predict the <b>growth behaviour</b>, not the exact time in seconds.</p>\n<h3>Example: counting operations</h3>\n<pre data-l=\"py\">\ndef sum_pairs(a):            # n = len(a)\n    total = 0                # 1 operation\n    for i in range(n):       # n times\n        for j in range(i+1, n):   # (n-1)+(n-2)+...+0 times\n            total += a[i]*a[j]    # c constant operations\n    return total\n</pre>\n<p>The inner loop body runs exactly $\\sum_{i=0}^{n-1}(n-1-i) = \\frac{n(n-1)}{2}$ times. So $T(n)=c\\cdot\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}+O(n)$, which is of order $n^2$. Note that the factor $\\frac12$ and the lower-order terms become irrelevant in the long-run behaviour.</p>\n<h2>3. Space complexity</h2>\n<p><b>Space complexity</b> is the amount of memory the algorithm uses beyond the input (<i>auxiliary space</i>). The memory of the recursive call stack counts too: a recursive function of depth $n$ needs $O(n)$ memory even if it has no helper variables. There is usually a trade-off between time and memory; for example a hash table gives faster lookup in exchange for more memory.</p>\n<h2>4. Asymptotic notation</h2>\n<p>Let $f,g:\\mathbb{N}\\to\\mathbb{R}_{\\ge0}$.</p>\n$$f(n)=O(g(n))\\iff \\exists\\,c>0,\\,n_0:\\ \\forall n\\ge n_0,\\ f(n)\\le c\\,g(n)$$\n$$f(n)=\\Omega(g(n))\\iff \\exists\\,c>0,\\,n_0:\\ \\forall n\\ge n_0,\\ f(n)\\ge c\\,g(n)$$\n$$f(n)=\\Theta(g(n))\\iff f=O(g)\\ \\text{and}\\ f=\\Omega(g)$$\n<p><b>Proof example:</b> we show $3n^2+5n+2=\\Theta(n^2)$. For $n\\ge1$ we have $3n^2\\le 3n^2+5n+2\\le 3n^2+5n^2+2n^2=10n^2$. So with $c_1=3$, $c_2=10$ and $n_0=1$ both sides hold.</p>\n<div class=\"callout warn\"><b class=\"t\">Careful: Big-O is not the same as \"worst case\" or \"running time\"</b>$O(g)$ is only an <b>upper bound on a function</b> and can be applied separately to the best, average or worst case. The sentence \"this algorithm is $O(n^2)$\" does not by itself say what the exact time is; it only says its growth is no more than $n^2$. When we want a tight bound we use $\\Theta$.</div>\n<h2>5. Best, average and worst case</h2>\n<p>For inputs of the same size $n$ the running time can differ. The <b>best case</b> is the minimum, the <b>worst case</b> the maximum and the <b>average case</b> the expected time under an assumed distribution over inputs. In linear search: the best case is 1 comparison ($\\Theta(1)$), the worst case $n$ comparisons and the average (assuming a uniformly random target position) $\\frac{n+1}{2}$.</p>\n<div class=\"wbox\" data-w=\"linsearch\" data-t=\"Linear search: best / average / worst case\" data-o=\"{}\"></div>\n<h2>6. Comparing growth rates</h2>\n<p>The usual order of growth: $1\\ll\\log n\\ll\\sqrt n\\ll n\\ll n\\log n\\ll n^2\\ll n^3\\ll 2^n\\ll n!$. The table and chart below show why exponential algorithms are practical only for very small inputs.</p>\n<div class=\"wbox\" data-w=\"cplx\" data-t=\"Growth of complexity functions and approximate running time\" data-o=\"{}\"></div>\n<h3>Practical rules for analysing code</h3>\n<ul><li><b>Constants and lower-order terms are dropped:</b> $5n^2+100n+7=\\Theta(n^2)$.</li><li><b>A sequence of statements:</b> costs add and the largest order dominates.</li><li><b>Nested loops:</b> costs (when independent) multiply.</li><li><b>Repeated halving</b> (as in binary search) makes the number of steps $\\lfloor\\log_2 n\\rfloor+1$.</li><li>The base of the logarithm does not matter in asymptotic notation: $\\log_a n=\\Theta(\\log_b n)$.</li></ul>\n<h2>7. Limits of asymptotic analysis</h2>\n<p>For small $n$ or large constants, an algorithm that is \"worse\" in order can be faster (for example insertion sort on a small array beats merge sort; that is why real sort implementations are hybrids). Asymptotic analysis also does not model the effect of the cache, I/O and parallelism.</p>", "obj": ["Define an algorithm precisely and state the difference between correctness and efficiency.", "Derive the time and space complexity of a code fragment by counting basic operations.", "Explain the notations $O$, $\\Omega$ and $\\Theta$ with their mathematical definitions and know how they differ from best/worst case.", "Compare the growth of common functions and estimate whether an algorithm is feasible for large inputs."], "pre": ["Familiarity with loops, functions and arrays in a programming language"], "mis": ["\"$O(n)$ means exactly n steps.\" ← No; it means of order $n$, without constant factors. $100n$ and $n/2$ are both $O(n)$.", "\"An $O(n^2)$ algorithm is always slower than an $O(n)$ one.\" ← Only for large enough $n$. For small $n$ the constants decide.", "\"Big-O = worst case.\" ← The two are independent concepts; $O$ is a bound on a function and the worst case is a kind of input.", "\"Space complexity means the input size.\" ← We usually count only the auxiliary memory."], "sum": ["Complexity is a function of the input size that measures growth behaviour independently of the hardware.", "$O$ is an upper bound, $\\Omega$ a lower bound and $\\Theta$ a tight bound; best/average/worst identify the kind of input.", "Constants and lower-order terms vanish in asymptotic notation but can matter in practice.", "Space complexity includes auxiliary memory and the recursion stack."], "ex": [{"q": "Which statement about $f(n)=4n^2+10n+1$ is true?", "e": "The dominant term is $4n^2$ and the constant $4$ is dropped; so $\\Theta(n^2)$. Note: $f=O(n^3)$ is also true but not a tight bound.", "o": ["$f(n)=\\Theta(n)$", "$f(n)=\\Theta(n^2)$", "$f(n)=\\Theta(n^3)$", "$f(n)=\\Theta(2^n)$"]}, {"q": "If an algorithm is $O(n^2)$, it runs slower than an $O(n)$ algorithm for every input.", "e": "The notation $O$ describes asymptotic behaviour and ignores constants. For small $n$ or favourable inputs a higher-order algorithm can be faster."}, {"q": "For $n=10$, how many times does the inner loop of the code below run?<pre data-l=\"py\">for i in range(1, n+1):\n    for j in range(1, i+1):\n        work()</pre>", "e": "The number of executions is $\\sum_{i=1}^{n} i=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}$. For $n=10$: $\\frac{10\\cdot 11}{2}=55$."}, {"q": "How many comparisons does binary search need at most, in the worst case, on a sorted array of 1024 elements? (formula $\\lfloor\\log_2 n\\rfloor+1$)", "e": "$\\log_2 1024=10$ so $10+1=11$ comparisons in the worst case."}, {"q": "How does $f(n)=n\\log n$ relate to $g(n)=n^2$?", "e": "$\\lim \\frac{n\\log n}{n^2}=\\lim\\frac{\\log n}{n}=0$ so $f=o(g)$; that is, $f=O(g)$ but $f$ grows more slowly than $g$ and is not $\\Omega(g)$.", "o": ["$f=\\Omega(g)$ but $f\\neq O(g)$", "$f=O(g)$ but $f\\neq\\Omega(g)$", "$f=\\Theta(g)$", "They are not comparable"]}, {"q": "Explain the difference between \"worst case\" and \"$O$\" with an example. Why is the statement \"insertion sort's best-case time is $O(n)$\" meaningful?", "a": "The worst case specifies which input we examine (the worst one), whereas $O$ specifies what kind of bound (upper) we derive for the time function. We can state an upper bound for the best case too. In insertion sort on an already-sorted input the inner loop always stops immediately and $T_{best}(n)=\\Theta(n)$; so \"the best case is $O(n)$\" means even on the best input the growth is no more than linear (and since it is also $\\Omega(n)$, it is $\\Theta(n)$)."}]});
addEN("a1-2",{"body": "\n<h2>The problem</h2>\n<p>Sometimes an operation is cheap most of the time and very expensive rarely. If we count only the worst case of that single operation we are too pessimistic. <b>Amortized analysis</b> measures the total cost of a <b>sequence</b> of $n$ operations in the <b>worst sequence</b> and divides by $n$. It is a deterministic guarantee for the sequence of operations, not a probabilistic analysis.</p>\n<h2>Main example: the dynamic array</h2>\n<p>When the array is full we build a new array of double the capacity and copy all the elements (cost $\\Theta(k)$ when copying $k$ elements). An ordinary push is $\\Theta(1)$.</p>\n<h3>The aggregate method</h3>\n<p>In $n$ push operations the copies happen at sizes $1,2,4,\\dots,2^{\\lfloor\\log_2 n\\rfloor}$. The total cost of the copies:</p>\n$$\\sum_{i=0}^{\\lfloor\\log_2 n\\rfloor}2^i<2n$$\n<p>So the total cost is $\\le n+2n=3n$ and the amortized cost of each push is below 3, i.e. $O(1)$.</p>\n<h3>The accounting method</h3>\n<p>We charge each push an amortized cost of 3 \"coins\": 1 coin for the insertion itself, 1 coin for copying this same element at the next growth, and 1 coin as savings for copying one of the older half's elements. When a growth happens, the saved coins pay for the copying and the balance never goes negative.</p>\n<h3>The potential method</h3>\n<p>Define the potential function $\\Phi=2\\cdot\\text{size}-\\text{capacity}$. The amortized cost of operation $i$ is $\\hat c_i=c_i+\\Phi_i-\\Phi_{i-1}$. For a push without growth: $\\hat c=1+2=3$. For a push with growth (with $k$ old elements): $c=k+1$ and the potential goes from $2k-k=k$ to $2(k+1)-2k=2$, so $\\hat c=(k+1)+(2-k)=3$. Both cases give a constant.</p>\n<div class=\"wbox\" data-w=\"dynarr\" data-t=\"Dynamic-array simulator: doubling versus a fixed increase\" data-o=\"{}\"></div>\n<p>If the capacity grows by adding a fixed amount $c$ instead of multiplying by a constant factor, the number of copies is $\\Theta(n/c)$ and the total copying is $\\Theta(n^2/c)$; so the amortized cost is $\\Theta(n)$, not $O(1)$ — which is why geometric growth (×1.5 or ×2) is the standard.</p>\n<h2>Other applications</h2>\n<ul><li><b>Hash-table rehash:</b> when the load factor reaches the threshold the table doubles and all keys are rehashed; insertion is amortized $O(1)$.</li><li><b>Binary counter:</b> incrementing a $k$-bit counter flips amortized $O(1)$ bits.</li><li><b>Splay trees and union-find</b> are also analysed with amortized analysis.</li></ul>", "obj": ["Explain the difference between amortized analysis, average case and worst case.", "Derive the amortized cost of a sequence of operations with the aggregate, accounting and potential methods.", "Show why appending to a dynamic array by doubling the capacity is amortized $O(1)$."], "mis": ["\"Amortized means a probabilistic average.\" ← No; amortized is a guarantee on the worst sequence of operations with no probabilistic assumption. The average case depends on the input distribution.", "\"So no push is expensive.\" ← Individual operations can be $\\Theta(n)$ (a sudden latency spike); in real-time systems this matters even if the amortized cost is $O(1)$."], "sum": ["Amortized = the worst total cost of a sequence of operations divided by the number of operations, with no probabilistic assumption.", "The aggregate, accounting and potential methods give the same result.", "Geometric capacity growth makes push amortized $O(1)$; fixed additive growth does not."], "ex": [{"q": "In a doubling dynamic array with initial capacity 1, after 8 push operations how many elements in total were copied during growth? (copies occur at sizes 1, 2, 4)", "e": "Growth happens when full at sizes 1, 2 and 4: $1+2+4=7$ copies. (The growth at size 8 happens at the ninth push.)"}, {"q": "The amortized cost of a push in a doubling dynamic array is $O(1)$, but the worst case of one particular push is $\\Theta(n)$.", "e": "True; the pushes that trigger growth cost $\\Theta(n)$ but over the whole sequence the cost per push is a constant on average."}, {"q": "If the capacity grows by only 10 units at each growth, what happens to the amortized cost of a push?", "e": "The number of growths is $n/10$ and each growth copies $O(n)$ elements; the total is $\\Theta(n^2)$ and the per-operation average $\\Theta(n)$.", "o": ["$O(1)$", "$O(\\log n)$", "$O(n)$", "$O(n^2)$"]}, {"q": "Why is a growth factor of 1.5 preferred over 2 in some implementations? (Hint: reusing freed memory)", "a": "With factor 2 the sum of all previous block sizes is always less than the new block ($1+2+\\dots+2^{k-1}<2^k$), so the allocator can never combine the previously freed blocks for the next growth and reuse them. With a factor $\\le\\varphi\\approx1.618$ the sum of the earlier blocks eventually exceeds the new block and reuse becomes possible. The cost: more growths (a larger amortized constant).\n"}]});
addEN("a2-1",{"body": "\n<h2>1. The array</h2>\n<p>An array is a sequence of equally sized elements stored in a <b>contiguous block of memory</b>. If the start address is $base$ and each element has size $s$, the address of element $i$ is $base+i\\cdot s$: one multiplication and one addition, independent of $n$. This is the reason for <b>$O(1)$ random access</b>.</p>\n<p>But inserting or deleting in the middle of an array must shift the later elements: $O(n)$. A static array's size is fixed at creation. A <b>dynamic array</b> (like <code>list</code> in Python, <code>vector</code> in C++ and <code>ArrayList</code> in Java) makes appending at the end amortized $O(1)$ through geometric capacity growth (previous lesson).</p>\n<h2>2. The linked list</h2>\n<p>Each element is an independent <b>node</b> holding data and a pointer to the next node; nodes can be anywhere in memory.</p>\n<pre data-l=\"py\">\nclass Node:\n    def __init__(self, val, nxt=None):\n        self.val = val\n        self.next = nxt\n\ndef insert_head(head, val):      # O(1)\n    return Node(val, head)\n\ndef search(head, x):             # O(n)\n    cur = head\n    while cur and cur.val != x:\n        cur = cur.next\n    return cur\n\ndef reverse(head):               # O(n) time, O(1) memory\n    prev = None\n    while head:\n        head.next, prev, head = prev, head, head.next\n    return prev\n</pre>\n<p>In a <b>doubly linked list</b> each node also has a pointer to the previous node; traversal in both directions and deleting a known node in $O(1)$ become possible, at the cost of more memory. The <b>circular</b> variant links the end of the list back to the start.</p>\n<div class=\"wbox\" data-w=\"llist\" data-t=\"Linked list: insert, delete and search (counting steps)\" data-o=\"{}\"></div>\n<h2>3. Comparison</h2>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"dt fa\"><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Array</th><th>Dynamic array</th><th>Singly Linked List</th><th>Doubly Linked List</th></tr><tr><td>Access by index</td><td>$O(1)$</td><td>$O(1)$</td><td>$O(n)$</td><td>$O(n)$</td></tr><tr><td>Insert/delete at the start</td><td>$O(n)$</td><td>$O(n)$</td><td>$O(1)$</td><td>$O(1)$</td></tr><tr><td>Insert at the end</td><td>—</td><td>$O(1)$ amortized</td><td>$O(n)$ (with tail: $O(1)$)</td><td>$O(1)$ with tail</td></tr><tr><td>Insert/delete given the node</td><td>—</td><td>$O(n)$</td><td>Delete $O(n)$ (needs the previous node)</td><td>$O(1)$</td></tr><tr><td>Search by value</td><td>$O(n)$</td><td>$O(n)$</td><td>$O(n)$</td><td>$O(n)$</td></tr><tr><td>Memory overhead</td><td>Low</td><td>Low up to 2×</td><td>Next pointer</td><td>Two pointers</td></tr></table></div>\n<h2>4. Cache locality: why the array wins in practice</h2>\n<p>The processor reads data in 64-byte <b>cache lines</b>. Scanning an array gets neighbouring elements in one cache line (spatial locality) and the CPU prefetcher reads ahead. Linked-list nodes are scattered and every hop to the next node may be a cache miss (hundreds of cycles). For this reason, in many applications even middle insertions into a dynamic array are faster than in a linked list unless $n$ is very large.</p>\n<div class=\"callout tip\"><b class=\"t\">Where is a linked list really useful?</b>Implementing queues and deques, the free list in allocators, an LRU cache (doubly linked list + hash map), and structures that need merging/splitting lists without copying.</div>", "obj": ["Describe the array in memory as a contiguous block and give the reason for $O(1)$ access.", "Compare the cost of insert, delete and search in an array and in a linked list.", "Know the difference between singly and doubly linked lists and understand the effect of cache locality."], "mis": ["\"Insertion into a linked list is always $O(1)$.\" ← Only if you already have the insertion point (node). Finding the position is $O(n)$.", "\"A linked list is faster than an array because it has no shifting.\" ← Because of cache misses and many allocations it is often slower.", "\"Indexing Python lists is $O(n)$.\" ← A Python <code>list</code> is a dynamic array, not a linked list; access is $O(1)$."], "sum": ["Array: $O(1)$ access, $O(n)$ middle insert/delete, contiguous block, cache-friendly.", "Linked list: $O(1)$ insert/delete given the node, $O(n)$ access and search.", "A dynamic array has amortized $O(1)$ append.", "Hardware constants (cache) can cancel an asymptotic advantage."], "ex": [{"q": "Which operation on a singly linked list without a tail pointer is $O(1)$?", "e": "Inserting at the head only changes head. The others need traversal.", "o": ["Insert at the end", "Insert at the start", "Access the k-th element", "Delete the last node"]}, {"q": "In a static array, accessing the $i$-th element does not depend on $i$ and is $O(1)$.", "e": "The address is computed as $base+i\\cdot s$."}, {"q": "To insert one element at position 0 of an array with 1000 elements, at least how many elements must be shifted one place to the right?", "e": "All 1000 existing elements must move one place to make room for the new one."}, {"q": "What is the cost of deleting a given node $x$ (with a pointer to the node itself) from a doubly linked list?", "e": "With the prev and next pointers we can connect the neighbours directly: <code>x.prev.next=x.next; x.next.prev=x.prev</code>.", "o": ["$O(n)$", "$O(\\log n)$", "$O(1)$", "$O(n^2)$"]}, {"q": "A linear traversal of 10 million numbers is $O(n)$ in both an array and a linked list. Why can the real time differ by tens of times?", "a": "The array reads neighbouring elements in contiguous cache lines and the prefetcher loads ahead; almost all accesses are cache hits. In a linked list each node may be elsewhere in memory, the data dependency (the current node must be read to hop to the next) blocks prefetching and the pipeline, and many accesses are cache misses (hundreds of cycles). Asymptotic complexity does not see hardware constants."}]});
addEN("a2-2",{"body": "\n<h2>ADT and implementation</h2>\n<p>An <b>abstract data type (ADT)</b> specifies only the operations and behaviour, not how it is stored. Stack, queue and deque are three ADTs, each implementable with a dynamic array, a circular array or a linked list.</p>\n<h2>Stack — \"last in, first out\" (LIFO)</h2>\n<p>Operations: <code>push</code>, <code>pop</code>, <code>peek</code>, <code>isEmpty</code>; all $O(1)$ (amortized in a dynamic array). Like a pile of stacked dishes. Uses: the language <b>call stack</b>, undo/redo, evaluating postfix expressions, matching parentheses and non-recursive DFS.</p>\n<pre data-l=\"py\">\ndef balanced(s):\n    pairs = {')':'(', ']':'[', '}':'{'}\n    st = []\n    for ch in s:\n        if ch in '([{':\n            st.append(ch)\n        elif ch in pairs:\n            if not st or st.pop() != pairs[ch]:\n                return False\n    return not st            # O(n) time, O(n) memory\n</pre>\n<h2>Queue — \"first in, first out\" (FIFO)</h2>\n<p>Operations: <code>enqueue</code> (at the end), <code>dequeue</code> (from the front). If we build a queue with a plain array and remove from the front, the cost is $O(n)$; the solution is a <b>circular buffer</b> with two indices <code>head</code> and <code>tail</code> and modular arithmetic. Uses: BFS, process scheduling (round robin), network buffers, message queues (Kafka/RabbitMQ).</p>\n<pre data-l=\"py\">\nclass RingQueue:\n    def __init__(self, cap):\n        self.a = [None]*cap; self.head = 0; self.size = 0\n    def enqueue(self, x):\n        if self.size == len(self.a): raise OverflowError\n        self.a[(self.head + self.size) % len(self.a)] = x\n        self.size += 1\n    def dequeue(self):\n        x = self.a[self.head]\n        self.head = (self.head + 1) % len(self.a)\n        self.size -= 1\n        return x\n</pre>\n<h2>Deque — the double-ended queue</h2>\n<p>Insert and delete at both ends in $O(1)$. An important pattern is the <b>monotonic deque</b> for \"maximum in a sliding window\" in $O(n)$: we keep indices whose values decrease from front to back; an element smaller than the new one is removed from the back because it can never be the answer.</p>\n<div class=\"wbox\" data-w=\"stackq\" data-t=\"Stack / queue / deque simulator\" data-o=\"{}\"></div>\n<div class=\"callout note\"><b class=\"t\">Stack and recursion</b>Every function call pushes a \"frame\" onto the call stack. Any recursive algorithm can be made non-recursive with an explicit stack; and very deep recursion causes a <i>stack overflow</i>.</div>", "obj": ["Know the difference between an abstract data type (ADT) and an implementation.", "Implement stack, queue and deque with an array or a linked list.", "Solve the classic applications (parentheses, call stack, BFS, sliding window)."], "mis": ["\"Implement a queue with <code>list.pop(0)</code> in Python.\" ← It is $O(n)$; use <code>collections.deque</code>.", "\"Stack and queue are independent data structures.\" ← They are ADTs; their underlying storage is an array or a list.", "Forgetting to check underflow/overflow in a fixed-capacity implementation."], "sum": ["Stack (LIFO) and queue (FIFO) are ADTs and their main operations are $O(1)$.", "A circular buffer implements a queue without shifting.", "A deque offers both ends and the monotonic deque solves sliding-window problems in $O(n)$."], "ex": [{"q": "In what order does a program return from its functions (the call stack)?", "e": "The last function called returns first: LIFO.", "o": ["FIFO", "LIFO", "Random", "Priority"]}, {"q": "Evaluate the following postfix expression: <code>5 1 2 + 4 * + 3 -</code>", "e": "$1+2=3$; $3\\cdot4=12$; $5+12=17$; $17-3=14$. With a stack: operands are pushed and each operator pops the top two elements."}, {"q": "In a circular queue of capacity $n$, if we continue after 5 dequeues and 5 enqueues, we need to shift elements.", "e": "A circular buffer only updates the indices modulo the capacity and never shifts anything."}, {"q": "Which structure is used to solve \"the maximum of every window of size $k$\" in $O(n)$ time?", "e": "Each index enters and leaves at most once ⇒ $O(n)$. A heap would give $O(n\\log n)$.", "o": ["A plain stack", "Min-Heap", "Monotonic Deque", "Hash Table"]}, {"q": "Show how to implement a queue with two stacks and give the amortized cost of each operation.", "a": "Two stacks, in and out. enqueue: push onto in ($O(1)$). dequeue: if out is empty, pop all elements of in one by one and push them onto out (the order reverses and the front of the queue ends up on top of out), then pop from out. Each element moves to out at most once, so the total cost of $n$ operations is $O(n)$ and the amortized cost per operation is $O(1)$ (by the aggregate or potential method)."}]});
addEN("a2-3",{"body": "\n<h2>The idea</h2>\n<p>A <b>hash table</b> is a mapping from keys to values that performs lookup/insert/delete in $O(1)$ on average by converting the key into an array index. A <b>hash function</b> $h:\\text{Keys}\\to\\{0,\\dots,m-1\\}$ maps a key to one of $m$ slots. Since the key domain is much larger than $m$, by the <b>pigeonhole principle</b> collisions are unavoidable.</p>\n<h3>A good hash function</h3>\n<ul><li><b>Deterministic:</b> a key always gives the same output.</li><li><b>Uniform distribution:</b> it spreads keys roughly equally over the slots.</li><li><b>Fast:</b> cheap to compute.</li></ul>\n<p>String example: $h(s)=\\left(\\sum_{i} s_i\\cdot 31^{\\,len-1-i}\\right)\\bmod m$ (a polynomial with a prime base). Note: a hash-table hash function differs from a <i>cryptographic</i> hash function (SHA-256); the former needs speed and good spreading, the latter resistance to deliberate collisions.</p>\n<h2>Resolving collisions</h2>\n<h3>1. Separate chaining</h3>\n<p>Each slot is a list (chain) of the keys that land in it. The search cost is $\\Theta(1+\\alpha)$ where $\\alpha=n/m$ is the <b>load factor</b>. If the hash is \"simple uniform\" and $\\alpha=O(1)$ the average time is $O(1)$; but in the worst case (all keys in one slot) it becomes $O(n)$ (Java 8+ turns long chains into balanced trees).</p>\n<h3>2. Open addressing</h3>\n<p>All keys live inside the array itself. On a collision a probe sequence looks for an empty slot: <b>linear probing</b> ($h+i$), <b>quadratic</b> ($h+i^2$) and <b>double hashing</b> ($h_1+i\\,h_2$). Linear probing is cache-friendly but suffers <b>primary clustering</b>: contiguous clusters grow and search time explodes as $\\alpha$ approaches 1 (on average ≈ $\\frac12(1+\\frac1{(1-\\alpha)^2})$ for an unsuccessful search). Deletion must use a <b>tombstone</b>, otherwise the probe chain of other keys breaks.</p>\n<div class=\"wbox\" data-w=\"hash\" data-t=\"Hash-table simulator (chaining and linear probing)\" data-o=\"{}\"></div>\n<h2>Rehash and amortized analysis</h2>\n<p>When $\\alpha$ exceeds a threshold (say 0.75 in Java, 2/3 in Python), a table of double the size is built and all keys are rehashed (because $m$ changed). This operation costs $O(n)$ but, like the dynamic array, it adds up to amortized $O(1)$ per insertion.</p>\n<h2>Applications and limits</h2>\n<ul><li>Dictionary/map (<code>dict</code>, <code>HashMap</code>), set (<code>set</code>), frequency counting, removing duplicates, memoization, caches.</li><li>In databases: the <b>hash join</b> and the <b>hash index</b> (for equality, but not ranges).</li><li>Limit: key order is not preserved, range/min search is impossible, and keys must be <i>immutable</i> with consistent <code>equals/hash</code>.</li></ul>", "obj": ["Describe the inner mechanism of a hash table and the role of the hash function.", "Compare chaining and open addressing and explain clustering.", "Analyse the effect of the load factor and rehashing on performance."], "mis": ["\"Lookup in a hash table is always $O(1)$.\" ← On average, assuming a good hash; the worst case is $O(n)$.", "\"A hash table is sorted.\" ← The order is meaningless (Python ≥ 3.7 keeps insertion order for <code>dict</code> but that has nothing to do with the keys being sorted).", "\"When deleting a key in open addressing, just empty the slot.\" ← A tombstone must be left."], "sum": ["A hash table makes lookup/insert/delete $O(1)$ on average using a hash function; the worst case is $O(n)$.", "Collisions are unavoidable; chaining and open addressing are the two main remedies.", "The load factor $\\alpha$ and rehashing control performance; rehashing is amortized $O(1)$.", "A hash table has no order and no range search."], "ex": [{"q": "How is the load factor of a hash table defined?", "e": "$\\alpha=n/m$: the number of keys divided by the number of slots.", "o": ["$m/n$", "$n/m$", "$n\\cdot m$", "$\\log n$"]}, {"q": "With $h(k)=k\\bmod 7$ and linear probing, we insert the keys 15, 22 and 8 in this order into an empty table. At which index does key 8 end up?", "e": "$15\\bmod7=1$ ⇒ slot 1. $22\\bmod7=1$ collides ⇒ slot 2. $8\\bmod7=1$ collides with 1 and 2 ⇒ slot 3."}, {"q": "In open addressing with linear probing, deleting a key by emptying its slot is always safe.", "e": "If another key that collided was placed after it, searching for that key reaches an empty slot and wrongly says \"not found\". Tombstones are used."}, {"q": "What is the main advantage of chaining over open addressing?", "e": "In chaining the load factor can exceed 1 and it only slows down linearly; open addressing becomes very slow or full as $\\alpha\\to1$.", "o": ["Better cache locality", "More stable performance when $\\alpha>1$", "Less memory", "No need for a hash function"]}, {"q": "Why is the hash-table size usually chosen as a prime or a power of 2 combined with bit mixing? And what happens if the keys are multiples of $m$ with $h=k\\bmod m$?", "a": "If $m$ shares a factor with the key pattern (say keys are multiples of 10 and $m=100$) only some of the slots are used and collisions pile up. Primes share fewer factors with common patterns. A power of 2 makes the computation fast with a bitwise AND but sees only the low bits of the key; so the hash function must mix the bits well (mixing/finalizer).\n"}]});
addEN("a3-1",{"body": "\n<h2>1. The binary tree</h2>\n<p>A <b>tree</b> is a hierarchical structure of nodes: one <b>root</b>, every node has at most one parent and zero or more children; a node with no children is a <b>leaf</b>. The <b>depth</b> of a node = the number of edges to the root; the <b>height</b> of a tree = the maximum depth. In a <b>binary tree</b> every node has at most two children (left/right). A binary tree of height $h$ has at most $2^{h+1}-1$ nodes; so a \"full\" tree with $n$ nodes has height $\\log_2 n$.</p>\n<h3>Traversals</h3>\n<ul><li><b>Preorder</b> (root, left, right): suitable for copying/serializing a tree.</li><li><b>Inorder</b> (left, root, right): in a BST it returns the keys <b>in sorted order</b>.</li><li><b>Postorder</b> (left, right, root): suitable for deleting a tree and computing subtree sizes.</li><li><b>Level-order</b>: with a queue (BFS).</li></ul>\n<p>All are $\\Theta(n)$. A recursive traversal needs stack depth equal to the height.</p>\n<h2>2. The binary search tree (BST)</h2>\n<p>Property: for every node, all keys of the left subtree are <b>smaller</b> and all keys of the right subtree are <b>larger</b> than it. Search and insert compare the key and go left or right, with cost proportional to the <b>height</b>: $O(h)$. Deletion has three cases: a leaf (just remove), one child (replace with the child), two children (replace with the <i>inorder successor</i> = the smallest element of the right subtree, then delete it).</p>\n<pre data-l=\"py\">\ndef insert(node, key):\n    if node is None: return Node(key)\n    if key < node.key:  node.left  = insert(node.left,  key)\n    elif key > node.key: node.right = insert(node.right, key)\n    return node               # O(h)\n</pre>\n<p><b>The problem:</b> if keys are inserted in sorted order the BST turns into a linked list of height $n$ and all operations become $O(n)$. For a random BST the expected height is $O(\\log n)$ but this cannot be guaranteed for arbitrary input. The solution: <b>self-balancing trees</b>.</p>\n<div class=\"wbox\" data-w=\"tree\" data-t=\"Interactive BST: insert, delete, search and animated traversals\" data-o=\"{&quot;mode&quot;:&quot;bst&quot;}\"></div>\n<h2>3. The AVL tree</h2>\n<p>The <b>balance factor</b> of every node: $bf=h(\\text{left})-h(\\text{right})$. In an AVL tree $bf\\in\\{-1,0,1\\}$ must hold for all nodes. After an insert/delete, if $|bf|=2$ the tree is repaired with a <b>rotation</b>:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"dt fa\"><tr><th>Case</th><th>Condition</th><th>Fix</th></tr><tr><td>LL</td><td>Left-left imbalance</td><td>One right rotation</td></tr><tr><td>RR</td><td>Right-right imbalance</td><td>One left rotation</td></tr><tr><td>LR</td><td>Left-right imbalance</td><td>Left rotation on the left child, then a right rotation</td></tr><tr><td>RL</td><td>Right-left imbalance</td><td>Right rotation on the right child, then a left rotation</td></tr></table></div>\n<p>The height of an AVL tree with $n$ nodes is at most $1.44\\log_2 n$ (proved with the Fibonacci-like sequence of minimum node counts), so search/insert/delete are all $O(\\log n)$ and each insertion needs at most 2 rotations.</p>\n<div class=\"wbox\" data-w=\"tree\" data-t=\"Interactive AVL: balance factor and rotations\" data-o=\"{&quot;mode&quot;:&quot;avl&quot;}\"></div>\n<h2>4. The red-black tree</h2>\n<p>A binary search tree with node colouring (red/black) that maintains five properties: (1) every node is red or black; (2) the root is black; (3) the NIL leaves are black; (4) <b>the children of a red node are black</b>; (5) the number of black nodes on every path from the root to a NIL is the same (black-height). Consequence: the longest path is at most twice the shortest and the height is $\\le 2\\log_2(n+1)$.</p>\n<div class=\"wbox\" data-w=\"rbcheck\" data-t=\"Red-black property checker\" data-o=\"{}\"></div>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"dt fa\"><tr><th></th><th>AVL</th><th>Red-Black</th></tr><tr><td>Balance</td><td>Stricter (height ≤ 1.44 log n)</td><td>Looser (≤ 2 log n)</td></tr><tr><td>Search</td><td>Faster (shallower tree)</td><td>Slightly slower</td></tr><tr><td>Insert/delete</td><td>More rotations (especially on delete)</td><td>At most 2–3 rotations, many recolourings</td></tr><tr><td>Used for</td><td>Read-heavy data</td><td><code>std::map</code>, Java's <code>TreeMap</code>, the Linux scheduler</td></tr></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout note\"><b class=\"t\">Why don't databases use a BST?</b>Every BST node is a random access to memory/disk. High-fan-out trees like the B-tree are optimal for disk; you will see this in the database lessons and in the \"Connecting the Foundations\" section.</div>", "obj": ["Know the tree terms (root, leaf, depth, height) and the three traversals.", "Analyse BST operations together with the degenerate-tree problem.", "Explain balance using AVL rotations and the red-black properties."], "mis": ["\"A BST is always $O(\\log n)$.\" ← Only if it is balanced; the worst case is $O(n)$.", "\"Inorder gives sorted order on any binary tree.\" ← Only on a BST.", "\"A red-black tree is always faster than an AVL tree.\" ← It depends on the workload; AVL is better for many lookups."], "sum": ["A BST does its operations in $O(h)$; balancing keeps the height $O(\\log n)$.", "AVL guarantees balance with balance factors and rotations; red-black with colouring rules.", "Inorder on a BST gives sorted order."], "ex": [{"q": "What does the inorder traversal of a BST output?", "e": "Left (smaller) ← root ← right (larger) ⇒ ascending order.", "o": ["Keys in insertion order", "Keys in ascending order", "Keys in descending order", "Level by level"]}, {"q": "What is the maximum number of nodes of a binary tree of height $h=3$ (root at height 0)?"}, {"q": "We insert the keys 10, 20, 30 in this order into an empty AVL tree. Which rotation and what final root?", "e": "After inserting 30, node 10 has $bf=-2$ (case RR) ⇒ a left rotation and the new root is 20 with children 10 and 30.", "o": ["Right rotation, root 10", "Left rotation, root 20", "Two rotations, root 30", "No rotation, root 10"]}, {"q": "A red-black tree may contain two consecutive red nodes (parent and child).", "e": "Property (4) explicitly requires the children of a red node to be black."}, {"q": "Show why the minimum node count of an AVL tree of height $h$ satisfies $N(h)=N(h-1)+N(h-2)+1$ and what this implies for the height.", "a": "The AVL tree with fewest nodes and height $h$ must have a root where one subtree has height $h-1$ and the other (to have as few nodes as possible while keeping $|bf|\\le1$) has height $h-2$, and both are themselves minimal; so $N(h)=1+N(h-1)+N(h-2)$. This resembles Fibonacci and gives $N(h)=\\Omega(\\varphi^h)$; hence $h=O(\\log_\\varphi n)\\approx1.44\\log_2 n$."}]});
addEN("a3-2",{"body": "\n<h2>1. The binary heap</h2>\n<p>A <b>heap</b> is a <b>complete binary tree</b> (all levels full except possibly the last, which is filled from the left) with the \"heap property\": in a <b>min-heap</b> every node is less than or equal to its children (the reverse in a max-heap). Because the tree is complete it can be stored in an array without pointers (zero-based indices):</p>\n$$parent(i)=\\left\\lfloor\\frac{i-1}{2}\\right\\rfloor,\\quad left(i)=2i+1,\\quad right(i)=2i+2$$\n<ul><li><b>insert:</b> append at the end of the array and <b>sift-up</b> (swap with the parent until the property holds): $O(\\log n)$.</li><li><b>extract-min:</b> replace the root with the last element, remove the end and <b>sift-down</b> with the smaller child: $O(\\log n)$.</li><li><b>peek-min:</b> $O(1)$.</li><li><b>Searching for an arbitrary key:</b> $O(n)$ — a heap is not designed for searching.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"wbox\" data-w=\"heap\" data-t=\"Interactive heap with sift-up / sift-down animation\" data-o=\"{}\"></div>\n<h3>Build-heap in $O(n)$</h3>\n<p>Building a heap from $n$ elements by sift-down from the last parent to the root is $O(n)$, not $O(n\\log n)$: about half the nodes are leaves (cost 0), $n/4$ nodes make at most 1 swap, $n/8$ nodes at most 2, and so on.</p>\n$$T(n)\\le\\sum_{h=0}^{\\lfloor\\log n\\rfloor}\\left\\lceil\\frac{n}{2^{h+1}}\\right\\rceil O(h)=O\\!\\left(n\\sum_{h\\ge0}\\frac{h}{2^{h}}\\right)=O(2n)=O(n)$$\n<h3>The priority queue</h3>\n<p>An ADT with operations <code>insert(x, priority)</code> and <code>extractMin/Max</code>. A heap is the most common implementation. Uses: Dijkstra and Prim, task scheduling, <b>top-K</b> (a heap of size $k$ in $O(n\\log k)$), merging $k$ sorted lists, discrete-event simulation, heap sort.</p>\n<pre data-l=\"py\">\nimport heapq\ndef top_k(nums, k):                 # the k largest in O(n log k)\n    h = []\n    for x in nums:\n        heapq.heappush(h, x)\n        if len(h) > k: heapq.heappop(h)   # drop the smallest\n    return sorted(h, reverse=True)\n</pre>\n<h2>2. The trie (prefix tree)</h2>\n<p>A trie is a tree where each edge is a character and the path from the root to a node represents a <b>prefix</b>. A marked node means a word ends there. Insert, search and prefix check are $O(L)$ where $L$ is the key length, independent of the total number of words.</p>\n<pre data-l=\"py\">\nclass TrieNode:\n    def __init__(self): self.kids = {}; self.end = False\ndef insert(root, w):\n    n = root\n    for c in w:\n        n = n.kids.setdefault(c, TrieNode())\n    n.end = True\ndef starts_with(root, p):\n    n = root\n    for c in p:\n        if c not in n.kids: return None\n        n = n.kids[c]\n    return n          # From here all words in the subtree = autocomplete\n</pre>\n<div class=\"wbox\" data-w=\"trie\" data-t=\"Interactive trie and autocomplete\" data-o=\"{}\"></div>\n<p>Uses: autocomplete, spell checking, IP routing (longest prefix match, with the compressed form the radix tree), vocabulary indexing. <b>Cost:</b> lots of memory when sparsely populated (every node has a child table), reduced by compressed tries/radix trees or compact arrays.</p>", "obj": ["Explain the binary-heap structure in an array and the sift-up/down operations.", "Analyse build-heap in $O(n)$ and use the priority queue in applications.", "Implement a trie for prefix search and analyse its cost."], "mis": ["\"A heap is sorted.\" ← It only guarantees the parent–child relation; the order among siblings is unspecified.", "\"Build-heap is $n$ inserts and $O(n\\log n)$.\" ← With bottom-up sift-down it is $O(n)$.", "\"A trie is always faster and smaller than a hash table.\" ← It is great for prefixes but needs more memory."], "sum": ["A binary heap is a complete tree in an array; insert/extract $O(\\log n)$, peek $O(1)$, build $O(n)$.", "A priority queue is an ADT implemented with a heap; top-K and Dijkstra are the main uses.", "A trie gives prefix search in $O(L)$, at a memory cost."], "ex": [{"q": "In a min-heap stored in an array (indices from 0), what is the index of the parent of the element at index 9?"}, {"q": "The complexity of finding the smallest element in a min-heap:", "e": "The smallest element is always at the root (index 0).", "o": ["$O(1)$", "$O(\\log n)$", "$O(n)$", "$O(n\\log n)$"]}, {"q": "The best method to find the $k$ largest of $n$ elements with $O(k)$ memory:", "e": "A min-heap of size $k$: the smallest of the $k$ candidates is at the root and is replaced when a larger element arrives; cost $O(n\\log k)$.", "o": ["A full sort", "A min-heap of size $k$", "A max-heap of size $n$", "A hash table"]}, {"q": "Searching for a word of length $L$ in a trie depends on the total number of stored words.", "e": "The cost is $O(L)$, independent of the number of words."}, {"q": "Explain heap sort with its steps and complexity. Why is it often slower than quicksort in practice despite being $O(n\\log n)$?", "a": "Step 1: build a max-heap in $O(n)$. Step 2: $n$ times swap the root (maximum) with the last heap element, shrink the heap by one and sift-down: $O(n\\log n)$. At the end the array is sorted ascending with $O(1)$ auxiliary memory (in place). Practical slowness: the memory access pattern of sift-down jumps far between parent and child and is not cache-friendly; it also makes more comparisons and swaps than quicksort. It is not stable either."}]});
addEN("a3-3",{"body": "\n<h2>Definition</h2>\n<p>A graph $G=(V,E)$ is a set of <b>vertices</b> and <b>edges</b>. In an <b>undirected graph</b> the edge $\\{u,v\\}$ is two-way; in a <b>directed graph</b> the edge $(u\\to v)$ is one-way. In a <b>weighted graph</b> each edge has a number (cost, distance, capacity). Basic concepts:</p>\n<ul><li><b>Degree:</b> the number of edges attached to a vertex; in a directed graph the in-degree and out-degree.</li><li><b>Path:</b> a sequence of vertices where each two consecutive ones are joined by an edge; a <b>cycle</b> is a path that returns to its start vertex.</li><li><b>Connected:</b> there is a path between every two vertices. In directed graphs: <i>strongly connected (SCC)</i>.</li><li><b>DAG:</b> a directed acyclic graph; the model for task dependencies, build systems and neural-network computation graphs.</li><li>A <b>sparse</b> graph: $|E|\\approx|V|$; a <b>dense</b> graph: $|E|\\approx|V|^2$.</li></ul>\n<p>In a simple undirected graph: $\\sum_v \\deg(v)=2|E|$ (the handshake lemma) and $|E|\\le\\frac{|V|(|V|-1)}{2}$.</p>\n<h2>Representing a graph</h2>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"dt fa\"><tr><th>Representation</th><th>Memory</th><th>Check for edge $(u,v)$</th><th>Iterate the neighbours of $u$</th><th>Suited to</th></tr><tr><td>Adjacency List</td><td>$\\Theta(V+E)$</td><td>$O(\\deg u)$</td><td>$O(\\deg u)$</td><td>Sparse graphs (most of the real world)</td></tr><tr><td>Adjacency Matrix</td><td>$\\Theta(V^2)$</td><td>$O(1)$</td><td>$O(V)$</td><td>Dense graphs, Floyd–Warshall, matrix multiplication</td></tr><tr><td>Edge List</td><td>$\\Theta(E)$</td><td>$O(E)$</td><td>$O(E)$</td><td>Kruskal, Bellman–Ford, input/output</td></tr></table></div>\n<div class=\"wbox\" data-w=\"graphrep\" data-t=\"A graph in two representations; change direction and weights\" data-o=\"{}\"></div>\n<p>A social network with millions of users where everyone has a few hundred friends is extremely sparse; its adjacency matrix ($10^{12}$ cells) is impossible but an adjacency list is practical. In contrast, in a neural network or a similarity matrix a matrix is sometimes more natural. The <b>sparse matrix</b> representation (CSR/COO) bridges these two worlds.</p>\n<pre data-l=\"py\">\nfrom collections import defaultdict\ng = defaultdict(list)               # Adjacency List\nfor u, v, w in [('A','B',4), ('A','C',2), ('B','C',1)]:\n    g[u].append((v, w)); g[v].append((u, w))   # undirected: twice\n</pre>", "obj": ["Define the components of a graph and its types (directed, undirected, weighted, DAG).", "Compare the adjacency list, adjacency matrix and edge list representations.", "Choose a suitable representation for a problem according to the graph density."], "mis": ["\"The adjacency matrix is always simpler and better.\" ← It takes $\\Theta(V^2)$ memory even when there are few edges.", "Forgetting that in an undirected graph each edge is stored twice.", "Confusing \"connectedness\" in directed and undirected graphs."], "sum": ["A graph $G=(V,E)$ can be directed/undirected and weighted.", "An adjacency list suits sparse graphs ($\\Theta(V+E)$) and a matrix suits dense graphs or $O(1)$ edge access.", "Handshake lemma: the sum of the degrees $=2|E|$."], "ex": [{"q": "A simple undirected graph has 5 vertices with degrees 3, 2, 2, 2, 1. How many edges does it have?"}, {"q": "For a graph with $10^6$ vertices and $5\\times10^6$ edges, which representation is suitable?", "e": "The matrix would need $10^{12}$ cells; the adjacency list is $\\Theta(V+E)\\approx6\\times10^6$.", "o": ["Adjacency Matrix", "Adjacency List", "Both are the same", "Neither"]}, {"q": "A DAG may contain a cycle.", "e": "DAG means \"directed acyclic graph\"; no cycles."}, {"q": "The maximum number of edges in a simple directed graph with $n$ vertices (no self-loops):", "e": "One edge for every ordered pair $(u,v)$ with $u\\neq v$: $n(n-1)$.", "o": ["$n(n-1)/2$", "$n(n-1)$", "$n^2$", "$2n$"]}, {"q": "How would you store a weighted city road network with an adjacency list, and which operations are slow on it?", "a": "For each intersection a list of (neighbour, weight) pairs. Iterating the neighbours is $O(\\deg)$ and memory is $\\Theta(V+E)$. Directly checking whether a specific edge $(u,v)$ exists, or finding its weight, is $O(\\deg u)$ (a hash table per list makes it $O(1)$ on average). A road graph is sparse (each intersection has few streets), so an adjacency list is better than a matrix.\n"}]});
addEN("a4-1",{"body": "\n<h2>1. Searching</h2>\n<p><b>Linear search</b> on unsorted data is $O(n)$. If the array is <b>sorted</b>, <b>binary search</b> discards half the range with every comparison: $T(n)=T(n/2)+O(1)\\Rightarrow O(\\log n)$.</p>\n<pre data-l=\"py\">\ndef binary_search(a, t):\n    lo, hi = 0, len(a) - 1\n    while lo <= hi:                  # invariant: if t is in a, it is in a[lo..hi]\n        mid = lo + (hi - lo) // 2    # not (lo+hi)//2 ; avoids overflow in languages with fixed-width integers\n        if a[mid] == t: return mid\n        if a[mid] < t:  lo = mid + 1\n        else:           hi = mid - 1\n    return -1\n</pre>\n<div class=\"wbox\" data-w=\"bsearch\" data-t=\"Binary search step by step\" data-o=\"{}\"></div>\n<p>Common mistakes: <code>lo &lt; hi</code> instead of <code>lo &lt;= hi</code> (missing the last element), updating <code>lo = mid</code> (infinite loop), forgetting that the input must be sorted. Generalizations: lower_bound/upper_bound and \"binary search on the answer\" (for example the smallest capacity that satisfies a condition).</p>\n<h2>2. Sorting</h2>\n<p>Important properties: <b>stable</b> means equal elements keep their original relative order (needed for multi-key sorting); <b>in-place</b> means $O(1)$ or $O(\\log n)$ auxiliary memory.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"dt fa\"><tr><th>Algorithm</th><th>Best</th><th>Average</th><th>Worst</th><th>Memory</th><th>Stable?</th><th>Note</th></tr><tr><td>Bubble</td><td>$O(n)$</td><td>$O(n^2)$</td><td>$O(n^2)$</td><td>$O(1)$</td><td>Yes</td><td>Educational; with an early-exit flag</td></tr><tr><td>Selection</td><td>$O(n^2)$</td><td>$O(n^2)$</td><td>$O(n^2)$</td><td>$O(1)$</td><td>No</td><td>Fewest swaps ($n-1$)</td></tr><tr><td>Insertion</td><td>$O(n)$</td><td>$O(n^2)$</td><td>$O(n^2)$</td><td>$O(1)$</td><td>Yes</td><td>Great for small or nearly sorted data</td></tr><tr><td>Merge</td><td>$O(n\\log n)$</td><td>$O(n\\log n)$</td><td>$O(n\\log n)$</td><td>$O(n)$</td><td>Yes</td><td>Divide and conquer; suited to linked lists and external data</td></tr><tr><td>Quick</td><td>$O(n\\log n)$</td><td>$O(n\\log n)$</td><td>$O(n^2)$</td><td>$O(\\log n)$</td><td>No</td><td>Fast in practice; the pivot choice matters</td></tr><tr><td>Heap</td><td>$O(n\\log n)$</td><td>$O(n\\log n)$</td><td>$O(n\\log n)$</td><td>$O(1)$</td><td>No</td><td>Guaranteed worst case, but poor cache behaviour</td></tr></table></div>\n<h3>Merge Sort</h3>\n<p>Splits the array in half, sorts each half recursively and merges the two sorted halves in $O(n)$: $T(n)=2T(n/2)+\\Theta(n)\\Rightarrow\\Theta(n\\log n)$ in all cases.</p>\n<pre data-l=\"py\">\ndef merge_sort(a):\n    if len(a) <= 1: return a\n    m = len(a)//2\n    L, R = merge_sort(a[:m]), merge_sort(a[m:])\n    out, i, j = [], 0, 0\n    while i < len(L) and j < len(R):\n        if L[i] <= R[j]: out.append(L[i]); i += 1    # &lt;= for stability\n        else:            out.append(R[j]); j += 1\n    return out + L[i:] + R[j:]\n</pre>\n<h3>Quick Sort</h3>\n<p>Chooses a <b>pivot</b>, partitions the array into \"smaller\" and \"larger\" and sorts the two parts recursively. If the pivot is always near the median $T(n)=2T(n/2)+\\Theta(n)$ and the cost is $\\Theta(n\\log n)$; if it is always the smallest/largest (for example a sorted array with the last element as pivot) $T(n)=T(n-1)+\\Theta(n)=\\Theta(n^2)$. With a random pivot or median-of-three the expected time is $O(n\\log n)$.</p>\n<div class=\"wbox\" data-w=\"sort\" data-t=\"Sorting animation; change the algorithm and the data type and compare the number of comparisons\" data-o=\"{}\"></div>\n<h2>3. The lower bound for comparison sorting</h2>\n<p>Every comparison-based algorithm can be modelled by a binary <b>decision tree</b> in which each leaf is one of the $n!$ possible permutations. A binary tree with $n!$ leaves has height at least $\\log_2(n!)$ and by Stirling's approximation $\\log_2(n!)=\\Theta(n\\log n)$. So no comparison-based algorithm can do better than $\\Omega(n\\log n)$ in the worst case. Algorithms like <b>counting sort</b> and <b>radix sort</b> escape this bound by using the structure of the keys (integers in a small range): counting sort runs in $O(n+k)$.</p>\n<div class=\"callout tip\"><b class=\"t\">In practice</b>Standard libraries usually use hybrid algorithms: Timsort (Python/Java for objects: a combination of merge and insertion sort that detects sorted runs) and Introsort (C++: quicksort that switches to heap sort when the depth gets large). You do not need to write your own sort except for learning.</div>", "obj": ["Implement binary search correctly with a loop invariant and know its common mistakes.", "Compare merge sort, quicksort and heap sort (time, memory, stability).", "Explain the $\\Omega(n\\log n)$ lower bound of comparison sorting."], "mis": ["\"Quicksort is always $O(n\\log n)$.\" ← On average; the worst case is $O(n^2)$.", "\"Merge sort is in place.\" ← The usual version needs $O(n)$ auxiliary memory.", "\"Binary search also works on an unsorted array.\" ← No; sortedness is a precondition.", "\"A stable sort means faster.\" ← Stability is a property of order, not speed."], "sum": ["Binary search on sorted data is $O(\\log n)$; the loop invariant and overflow avoidance matter.", "Merge sort is stable and $\\Theta(n\\log n)$ with $O(n)$ memory; quicksort is fastest in practice but has a worst case of $O(n^2)$; heap sort is in place with a worst-case guarantee.", "Comparison sorting cannot beat $\\Omega(n\\log n)$."], "ex": [{"q": "What is the maximum number of comparisons of binary search on a sorted array of 15 elements?"}, {"q": "Which sorting algorithm is $O(n\\log n)$ even in the worst case and in place?", "e": "Heap sort has a worst case of $O(n\\log n)$ and $O(1)$ auxiliary memory. Merge sort needs $O(n)$ memory and quicksort has a worst case of $O(n^2)$.", "o": ["Merge Sort", "Quick Sort", "Heap Sort", "Insertion Sort"]}, {"q": "Insertion sort on an already sorted array is $O(n)$.", "e": "Each element is compared only with the previous one and nothing moves: $n-1$ comparisons."}, {"q": "How does quicksort with pivot = the last element behave on a sorted ascending array?", "e": "The pivot is always the largest; the partition is unbalanced ($n-1$ and 0) ⇒ $T(n)=T(n-1)+n=\\Theta(n^2)$.", "o": ["$O(n\\log n)$", "$O(n)$", "$O(n^2)$", "$O(n^3)$"]}, {"q": "Why can't we build a \"comparison\" sorting algorithm with $O(n)$ worst-case comparisons? (decision-tree argument)", "a": "To identify one of the $n!$ input permutations the algorithm needs at least $n!$ distinct leaves in its decision tree. A binary tree with $L$ leaves has height at least $\\log_2 L$, so the worst-case comparisons are $\\ge\\log_2(n!)$. By Stirling $\\ln n!\\approx n\\ln n-n$ ⇒ $\\log_2 n!=\\Theta(n\\log n)$. Therefore the bound is $\\Omega(n\\log n)$ and $O(n)$ is impossible."}]});
addEN("a4-2",{"body": "\n<h2>The concept</h2>\n<p>Graph traversal means visiting, in an orderly way, all vertices reachable from a start vertex. To avoid looping forever in cycles we keep a <b>visited</b> set. The difference between BFS and DFS is only the data structure of the \"frontier\": a <b>queue (FIFO)</b> or a <b>stack (LIFO)</b>.</p>\n<h2>BFS — breadth-first search</h2>\n<pre data-l=\"py\">\nfrom collections import deque\ndef bfs(g, s):\n    dist = {s: 0}; q = deque([s])\n    while q:\n        u = q.popleft()\n        for v in g[u]:\n            if v not in dist:          # mark when entering the queue\n                dist[v] = dist[u] + 1\n                q.append(v)\n    return dist                        # time O(V+E), memory O(V)\n</pre>\n<p>BFS sees vertices in order of their distance (number of edges) from the source, so on an <b>unweighted graph</b> it gives the shortest path. Why it is correct: the queue always holds vertices at distance $d$ before those at $d+1$ (an invariant).</p>\n<h2>DFS — depth-first search</h2>\n<pre data-l=\"py\">\ndef dfs(g, u, seen, order):\n    seen.add(u)\n    for v in g[u]:\n        if v not in seen:\n            dfs(g, v, seen, order)\n    order.append(u)                   # post-order: u is finished\n</pre>\n<p>DFS goes as deep as possible and then returns (backtracks). By recording the entry/exit time of each vertex, edge types (tree, back, forward, cross) are classified; a <b>back edge</b> means a cycle. The reverse of DFS post-order on a DAG is a <b>topological order</b>.</p>\n<div class=\"wbox\" data-w=\"graphalgo\" data-t=\"BFS and DFS animation (change the start vertex)\" data-o=\"{&quot;algos&quot;:[&quot;bfs&quot;,&quot;dfs&quot;]}\"></div>\n<h2>Complexity</h2>\n<p>Each vertex is examined once and each edge (twice in an undirected graph) once: time $\\Theta(V+E)$ with an adjacency list (and $\\Theta(V^2)$ with an adjacency matrix). BFS memory is up to $O(V)$ (the width of the graph), DFS memory up to $O(V)$ (the depth).</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"dt fa\"><tr><th></th><th>BFS</th><th>DFS</th></tr><tr><td>Structure</td><td>Queue</td><td>Stack/recursion</td></tr><tr><td>Shortest path (unweighted)</td><td>✅ guaranteed</td><td>❌</td></tr><tr><td>Cycle detection, topological sort, SCC</td><td>Hard</td><td>✅ natural</td></tr><tr><td>Memory on a wide graph</td><td>High</td><td>Low</td></tr><tr><td>Memory on a deep graph</td><td>Low</td><td>High (stack overflow risk)</td></tr></table></div>\n<h3>Applications</h3>\n<ul><li>Connected components, checking bipartiteness (BFS with two colours).</li><li>Solving puzzles and flood fill (BFS on a grid).</li><li>Topological sort for scheduling dependencies (builds, course prerequisites).</li><li>Web crawlers (BFS), deadlock detection in the wait-for graph (DFS).</li></ul>", "obj": ["Implement BFS and DFS with a queue and a stack and compare their behaviour.", "Explain the $O(V+E)$ complexity.", "Apply BFS for shortest paths in unweighted graphs and DFS for cycle detection and topological sort."], "mis": ["\"BFS on a weighted graph gives the shortest path.\" ← Only when all weights are equal; otherwise use Dijkstra.", "Marking visited when \"dequeuing\" (not when enqueuing) causes many repeated entries and wrong distances.", "\"The DFS visit order is unique.\" ← It depends on the order of the neighbours."], "sum": ["BFS (queue) and DFS (stack) are both $\\Theta(V+E)$ with an adjacency list.", "BFS gives unweighted shortest paths; DFS suits cycles, topological sort and SCC.", "The visited mark must be set when a vertex is added to the frontier (BFS)."], "ex": [{"q": "Which auxiliary data structure does BFS use?", "e": "BFS uses a FIFO queue.", "o": ["Stack", "Queue", "Heap", "Trie"]}, {"q": "In an undirected graph with 100 vertices and 300 edges (adjacency list), at most how many times does BFS \"examine a neighbour\"? (each edge from both sides)", "e": "The sum of the adjacency-list lengths $=2|E|=600$."}, {"q": "In a directed graph, a back edge in DFS indicates a cycle.", "e": "A back edge goes to an ancestor of the current vertex on the stack and forms a cycle."}, {"q": "Which do you choose to find the shortest path (by number of edges) in a grid maze?", "e": "BFS visits vertices in order of distance.", "o": ["DFS", "BFS", "Heap Sort", "Binary Search"]}, {"q": "Describe the topological-sort algorithm with DFS and say what happens if the graph has a cycle.", "a": "Run DFS from every unvisited vertex; append each vertex to a list when its processing finishes (post-order). The reverse of the list is a topological order because for an edge $u\\to v$, $v$ always finishes before $u$. If DFS reaches a vertex that is on the current stack (grey) a back edge has been found, the graph has a cycle and no topological order exists."}]});
addEN("a4-3",{"body": "\n<h2>Relaxation</h2>\n<p>All the algorithms in this section rely on the <b>relax</b> operation: if reaching $v$ through $u$ is cheaper, we improve the recorded distance:</p>\n$$\\text{if } d[u]+w(u,v)<d[v]:\\ d[v]\\leftarrow d[u]+w(u,v),\\ \\pi[v]\\leftarrow u$$\n<p>The difference between the algorithms is the \"order\" and \"number of times\" of relaxing.</p>\n<h2>Dijkstra — single source, non-negative weights</h2>\n<p>A <b>greedy algorithm</b>: it always takes the closest unfinalized vertex, finalizes it and relaxes its edges. Why it is correct: with non-negative weights the shortest path to the vertex that currently has the smallest $d$ cannot be shorter than a path through another unfinalized vertex. With a negative edge this argument breaks.</p>\n<pre data-l=\"py\">\nimport heapq\ndef dijkstra(g, s):\n    dist = {s: 0}; pq = [(0, s)]\n    while pq:\n        d, u = heapq.heappop(pq)\n        if d > dist.get(u, float('inf')): continue    # stale entry (lazy deletion)\n        for v, w in g[u]:\n            nd = d + w\n            if nd < dist.get(v, float('inf')):\n                dist[v] = nd; heapq.heappush(pq, (nd, v))\n    return dist\n</pre>\n<p>Complexity: $O(V^2)$ with an array; $O((V+E)\\log V)$ with a binary heap; $O(E+V\\log V)$ with a Fibonacci heap. The choice of the priority-queue data structure changes the algorithm's complexity.</p>\n<h2>Bellman–Ford — negative edges and negative-cycle detection</h2>\n<p>It relaxes all edges $V-1$ times. Since a shortest simple path has at most $V-1$ edges, the distances are final after $V-1$ rounds. If an improvement still occurs in round $V$, a reachable <b>negative cycle</b> exists and the shortest path is undefined (it can be decreased to $-\\infty$). Complexity $O(VE)$.</p>\n<div class=\"wbox\" data-w=\"graphalgo\" data-t=\"Dijkstra and Bellman–Ford on the sample graph (Bellman–Ford on a directed graph with a negative weight)\" data-o=\"{&quot;algos&quot;:[&quot;dijkstra&quot;,&quot;bellman&quot;]}\"></div>\n<h2>Floyd–Warshall — all pairs</h2>\n<p>A <b>dynamic-programming</b> algorithm: let $D^{(k)}_{ij}$ be the shortest path $i\\to j$ whose intermediate vertices come only from $\\{1..k\\}$. Then:</p>\n$$D^{(k)}_{ij}=\\min\\left(D^{(k-1)}_{ij},\\ D^{(k-1)}_{ik}+D^{(k-1)}_{kj}\\right)$$\n<p>That is, either the path does not pass through $k$, or it passes through $k$ and splits into two smaller subproblems. Three nested loops: $\\Theta(V^3)$ time and $\\Theta(V^2)$ memory (updated in place). Negative weights are allowed (without negative cycles; if $D_{ii}<0$ there is a negative cycle).</p>\n<div class=\"wbox\" data-w=\"floyd\" data-t=\"Floyd–Warshall: the distance matrix at each step k\" data-o=\"{}\"></div>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"dt fa\"><tr><th>Algorithm</th><th>Problem type</th><th>Negative weights?</th><th>Time</th><th>Used for</th></tr><tr><td>BFS</td><td>Single source, unweighted</td><td>—</td><td>$O(V+E)$</td><td>Simple networks, mazes</td></tr><tr><td>Dijkstra</td><td>Single source</td><td>❌</td><td>$O((V+E)\\log V)$</td><td>Map routing, OSPF</td></tr><tr><td>Bellman-Ford</td><td>Single source</td><td>✅ (detects negative cycles)</td><td>$O(VE)$</td><td>RIP, currency arbitrage</td></tr><tr><td>Floyd-Warshall</td><td>All pairs</td><td>✅</td><td>$O(V^3)$</td><td>Small dense graphs, transitive closure</td></tr></table></div>", "obj": ["Explain the idea of relaxation and compare the three algorithms by use and complexity.", "State why Dijkstra needs non-negative weights.", "Detect a negative cycle with Bellman–Ford and analyse Floyd–Warshall as DP."], "mis": ["\"Dijkstra also works with negative edges.\" ← No; a finalized vertex may improve later.", "\"Bellman–Ford is just slower than Dijkstra.\" ← Its advantage is supporting negative edges and detecting negative cycles.", "Without a negative cycle, and without defining it, people confuse \"distance −∞\" with an ordinary distance."], "sum": ["Relaxation is the core of all shortest-path algorithms.", "Dijkstra (greedy, non-negative weights, $O((V+E)\\log V)$), Bellman–Ford (negative weights and negative cycles, $O(VE)$), Floyd–Warshall (all pairs, DP, $O(V^3)$).", "The choice of priority-queue data structure sets Dijkstra's complexity."], "ex": [{"q": "Which algorithm correctly handles an edge with a negative weight (without a negative cycle)?", "e": "Bellman–Ford with repeated relaxation; Dijkstra assumes non-negative weights.", "o": ["Dijkstra", "Bellman-Ford", "BFS on a weighted graph", "None of them"]}, {"q": "What is the maximum number of relaxation rounds Bellman–Ford needs for $V=6$ vertices (not counting the extra negative-cycle-detection round)?", "e": "$V-1=5$ rounds are enough."}, {"q": "Dijkstra with a binary heap is faster than the array implementation on a sparse graph.", "e": "$O((V+E)\\log V)$ versus $O(V^2)$; when $E\\ll V^2$ the heap wins."}, {"q": "The memory complexity of Floyd–Warshall (with in-place updates):", "e": "One $V\\times V$ matrix is enough because $D^{(k)}$ can be written over $D^{(k-1)}$.", "o": ["$O(V)$", "$O(V\\log V)$", "$O(V^2)$", "$O(V^3)$"]}, {"q": "Build a counterexample with one negative edge on which Dijkstra gives a wrong answer.", "a": "Graph: $A\\to B$ with weight 2, $A\\to C$ with weight 5, $C\\to B$ with weight $-4$. Starting from $A$: Dijkstra first finalizes $B$ with distance 2 (the minimum of $\\{2,5\\}$) and never changes it; but the real path $A\\to C\\to B$ costs $5-4=1$. Dijkstra's answer (2) is wrong; Bellman–Ford correctly gives 1.\n"}]});
addEN("a5-1",{"body": "\n<h2>Recursion</h2>\n<p>A <b>recursive</b> function reduces a problem to smaller instances of <i>the same problem</i>. Every recursive solution needs two parts: a <b>base case</b> (finishes without a recursive call) and a <b>recursive step</b> that surely moves toward the base case. Otherwise it either runs forever or overflows the stack. Correctness is proved by <b>induction</b>.</p>\n<pre data-l=\"py\">\ndef power(x, n):                 # x^n by divide and conquer: O(log n)\n    if n == 0: return 1\n    half = power(x, n // 2)\n    return half*half if n % 2 == 0 else half*half*x\n</pre>\n<p>Each call has a frame on the call stack; the space complexity of a recursion is at least the recursion <i>depth</i>. <b>Tail recursion</b> (the recursive call is the function's last action) turns into a loop in languages with tail-call optimization (Python has none).</p>\n<h2>Divide and conquer</h2>\n<p>Three steps: <b>divide</b> (split into independent subproblems), <b>conquer</b> (solve recursively) and <b>combine</b> (merge the answers). Examples: merge sort, quicksort, binary search, Karatsuba multiplication, Strassen, the closest pair of points. The standard recurrence:</p>\n$$T(n)=a\\,T\\!\\left(\\frac nb\\right)+f(n)$$\n<p>$a$ subproblems each of size $n/b$ and a divide/combine cost $f(n)=\\Theta(n^d)$. The <b>master theorem</b> (for $f(n)=\\Theta(n^d)$) compares $d$ with $\\log_b a$:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"dt fa\"><tr><th>Case</th><th>Condition</th><th>Result</th><th>Intuition</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>$d<\\log_b a$</td><td>$\\Theta(n^{\\log_b a})$</td><td>The leaves' cost dominates</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>$d=\\log_b a$</td><td>$\\Theta(n^d\\log n)$</td><td>Every level of the tree costs the same</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>$d>\\log_b a$</td><td>$\\Theta(n^d)$</td><td>The root's cost dominates (with the regularity condition)</td></tr></table></div>\n<div class=\"wbox\" data-w=\"master\" data-t=\"Master-theorem calculator\" data-o=\"{}\"></div>\n<h2>Memoization</h2>\n<p>When subproblems <b>overlap</b> (like Fibonacci), the plain recursive solution recomputes the same subproblem many times and the time becomes exponential. <b>Memoization</b> stores each subproblem's result in a table (usually a dict) and returns it directly next time; the time drops to the number of distinct subproblems × the cost of each.</p>\n<pre data-l=\"py\">\nfrom functools import lru_cache\n@lru_cache(maxsize=None)\ndef fib(n):                       # O(n) time and memory  (without a cache: Θ(φ^n))\n    return n if n < 2 else fib(n-1) + fib(n-2)\n</pre>\n<div class=\"wbox\" data-w=\"fibtree\" data-t=\"The call tree of fib(n) without and with memoization\" data-o=\"{}\"></div>\n<div class=\"callout note\"><b class=\"t\">Memoization versus divide and conquer</b>Divide and conquer has <b>independent</b> subproblems (merge sort), so a cache is useless. Memoization/DP helps when subproblems <b>repeat</b>.</div>", "obj": ["Explain the structure of a recursive function (base case and recursive step) and the call stack.", "Write a recurrence and solve it with the master theorem.", "Apply memoization to remove repeated computation."], "mis": ["\"Recursion is always slower and worse than a loop.\" ← Sometimes it is inherent to the problem (trees, backtracking); what matters is depth and cost.", "\"Memoization needs no extra memory.\" ← The cache table and the recursion stack both use memory.", "Forgetting the base case or not approaching it."], "sum": ["Recursion = base case + a shrinking step; the recursion depth sets the space complexity.", "Divide and conquer is analysed with the recurrence $T(n)=aT(n/b)+f(n)$ and the master theorem.", "Memoization solves overlapping subproblems only once and turns exponential into polynomial."], "ex": [{"q": "For $T(n)=2T(n/2)+n$ with the master theorem: what is $\\log_b a$? (for $a=2,b=2$)", "e": "$\\log_2 2=1$ and $d=1$ ⇒ case 2: $\\Theta(n\\log n)$ (merge sort)."}, {"q": "$T(n)=T(n/2)+1$ (binary search) equals:", "e": "$a=1,b=2,d=0$; $\\log_2 1=0=d$ ⇒ case 2: $\\Theta(n^0\\log n)=\\Theta(\\log n)$.", "o": ["$\\Theta(n)$", "$\\Theta(\\log n)$", "$\\Theta(n\\log n)$", "$\\Theta(1)$"]}, {"q": "Roughly how many times does plain recursive fib(20) without a cache call the function? (see the widget calculator)", "e": "The number of calls is $2F(n+1)-1=2\\cdot10946-1=21891$. With memoization only 39 calls are needed."}, {"q": "What is $T(n)=3T(n/2)+n$?", "e": "$\\log_2 3\\approx1.585>d=1$ ⇒ case 1: $\\Theta(n^{\\log_2 3})$ (Karatsuba).", "o": ["$\\Theta(n\\log n)$", "$\\Theta(n^{\\log_2 3})\\approx n^{1.585}$", "$\\Theta(n^2)$", "$\\Theta(n)$"]}, {"q": "Show why plain recursive fib is exponential (the recurrence for the number of calls).", "a": "The number of calls is $C(n)=C(n-1)+C(n-2)+1$ with $C(0)=C(1)=1$. This is the Fibonacci sequence and $C(n)=\\Theta(\\varphi^n)$ with $\\varphi=\\frac{1+\\sqrt5}{2}\\approx1.618$. That is, each $n\\to n+1$ costs about 62% more work; the growth is exponential because fib($n-2$) is recomputed many times."}]});
addEN("a5-2",{"body": "\n<h2>Greedy algorithms</h2>\n<p>A greedy algorithm makes the \"best local choice\" at each step and never regrets. Its correctness depends on two properties: the <b>greedy-choice property</b> (a locally optimal choice fits inside a globally optimal solution) and <b>optimal substructure</b>. The proof is usually an <i>exchange argument</i>: any optimal solution can be transformed, without getting worse, into one that contains the greedy choice.</p>\n<p><b>Example: activity selection (interval scheduling)</b>: for the maximum number of non-overlapping intervals, sort the intervals by <i>finish</i> time and each time pick the compatible one that finishes earliest. Time $O(n\\log n)$. (Sorting by start time or by shortest length is <b>wrong</b>.) Other examples: Huffman, Kruskal/Prim, Dijkstra.</p>\n<div class=\"callout warn\"><b class=\"t\">Greedy is not always right</b>For coins $\\{1,3,4\\}$ and amount 6: greedy gives 4+1+1 (3 coins) but the optimal answer is 3+3 (2 coins). In \"canonical\" coin systems like $\\{1,5,10,25\\}$ greedy is optimal.</div>\n<div class=\"wbox\" data-w=\"coin\" data-t=\"Greedy versus DP on the coin problem\" data-o=\"{}\"></div>\n<h2>Dynamic programming</h2>\n<p>DP is for problems that (1) have <b>optimal substructure</b>: the optimal solution is built from optimal solutions of subproblems; and (2) have <b>overlapping subproblems</b>. Two implementation styles:</p>\n<ul><li><b>Top-down (memoization):</b> recursion + cache; builds only the needed subproblems.</li><li><b>Bottom-up (tabulation):</b> fills the table from small cases to large; no recursion overhead and memory can be reduced (keep only the previous row).</li></ul>\n<h3>How to solve a DP problem</h3>\n<ol><li>Define the <b>state</b>: what exactly does $dp[i][j]$ mean?</li><li>Write the <b>recurrence (transition)</b>.</li><li>Identify the <b>base cases</b>.</li><li>Choose the <b>fill order</b> so dependencies are already computed.</li><li>Read the answer from the table (and reconstruct with a traceback if needed).</li></ol>\n<h3>Example 1: the coin problem (minimum count)</h3>\n$$dp[x]=\\min_{c\\in coins,\\ c\\le x}\\left(dp[x-c]+1\\right),\\quad dp[0]=0$$\n<p>Time $O(x\\cdot|coins|)$ and memory $O(x)$.</p>\n<h3>Example 2: the longest common subsequence (LCS)</h3>\n<p>$dp[i][j]$ = the length of the LCS of the prefixes $X[1..i]$ and $Y[1..j]$:</p>\n$$dp[i][j]=\\begin{cases}dp[i-1][j-1]+1 & X_i=Y_j\\\\ \\max(dp[i-1][j],\\,dp[i][j-1]) & X_i\\ne Y_j\\end{cases}$$\n<div class=\"wbox\" data-w=\"lcs\" data-t=\"Filling the LCS table and the traceback\" data-o=\"{}\"></div>\n<h3>Example 3: the 0/1 knapsack</h3>\n<p>$dp[i][w]$ = the maximum value with the first $i$ items and capacity $w$: $dp[i][w]=\\max(dp[i-1][w],\\ dp[i-1][w-w_i]+v_i)$ (if $w_i\\le w$). Time $O(nW)$ — this is <b>pseudo-polynomial</b> because $W$ is a numeric value, not the input length; the problem is NP-hard.</p>\n<pre data-l=\"py\">\ndef knapsack(W, wt, val):\n    dp = [0]*(W+1)\n    for i in range(len(wt)):\n        for w in range(W, wt[i]-1, -1):      # reverse: each item once (0/1)\n            dp[w] = max(dp[w], dp[w-wt[i]] + val[i])\n    return dp[W]                              # memory O(W)\n</pre>", "obj": ["Check the greedy-choice and optimal-substructure properties.", "Solve DP problems by defining the state, the recurrence and the table fill order.", "Compare greedy and DP on the coin problem and compute an LCS."], "mis": ["\"Every optimization problem can be solved greedily.\" ← Only with the greedy-choice property; counterexample: coins {1,3,4}.", "\"DP means using a 2D array.\" ← DP is a <i>method</i>; the table is only its tool.", "\"DP is always polynomial.\" ← The knapsack $O(nW)$ is pseudo-polynomial."], "sum": ["Greedy: local choice + an exchange-argument proof; simple and fast but sometimes wrong.", "DP: state, recurrence, base cases, fill order; top-down (memoization) and bottom-up (tabulation).", "LCS in $O(mn)$; the 0/1 knapsack in pseudo-polynomial $O(nW)$."], "ex": [{"q": "In greedy interval scheduling, what are the intervals sorted by?", "e": "Sorting by finish time and picking the compatible interval that finishes earliest is optimal (exchange argument).", "o": ["Start time", "Finish time", "Interval length", "Random"]}, {"q": "For coins {1,3,4} and amount 6, what is the minimum number of coins by DP?", "e": "$dp[6]=\\min(dp[5],dp[3],dp[2])+1$; $dp[3]=1$ so $dp[6]=2$ (3+3)."}, {"q": "What is the LCS length of the strings \"ABCBDAB\" and \"BDCABA\"?", "e": "For example \"BCBA\" (or \"BDAB\") with length 4; the table in the LCS widget gives this value."}, {"q": "In the 0/1 knapsack with a 1D array, the capacity must be iterated from small to large.", "e": "It must be iterated from large to small so each item is used at most once; ascending iteration gives the unbounded knapsack."}, {"q": "Using the two DP properties, how do you tell whether a problem can be solved with greedy or needs DP? Argue for the coin problem.", "a": "Greedy needs the local choice never to block the way to the global solution. With coins {1,3,4} choosing 4 for 6 destroys the optimal answer (because the remainder 2 can only be filled with 1+1), so the greedy-choice property is violated. DP tries all options for the last coin ($\\min_c dp[x-c]+1$) and exploits optimal substructure and repeated subproblems. In canonical systems (every coin a multiple of the smaller one, or with a special property) one can prove greedy is correct."}]});
addEN("a5-3",{"body": "\n<h2>The idea</h2>\n<p><b>Backtracking</b> is depth-first search in a <b>state-space tree</b>: we build the solution step by step, and if a partial solution already violates the constraints we go back to the previous step without continuing that branch (<b>pruning</b>). The result: still exponential in the worst case but in practice many branches are never visited.</p>\n<h3>The general template</h3>\n<pre data-l=\"py\">\ndef solve(state, choices):\n    if is_complete(state):\n        record(state); return\n    for c in choices(state):\n        if is_valid(state, c):          # pruning: only consistent choices\n            apply(state, c)             # choose\n            solve(state, choices)       # explore\n            undo(state, c)              # un-choose  ← the heart of backtracking\n</pre>\n<h3>Example: all subsets and permutations</h3>\n<pre data-l=\"py\">\ndef permutations(a):\n    res, cur, used = [], [], [False]*len(a)\n    def go():\n        if len(cur) == len(a): res.append(cur[:]); return\n        for i in range(len(a)):\n            if not used[i]:\n                used[i] = True; cur.append(a[i])\n                go()\n                cur.pop(); used[i] = False\n    go(); return res                    # n! solutions ⇒ Θ(n·n!)\n</pre>\n<h3>Classic example: the N-queens problem</h3>\n<p>Place $N$ queens on an $N\\times N$ board so that no two share a row, column or diagonal. We place one queen per row and skip a column if it conflicts with earlier queens. Without pruning there are $N^N$ states; with pruning for $N=6$ only about 900 cells are tried (instead of $6^6=46656$).</p>\n<div class=\"wbox\" data-w=\"queens\" data-t=\"N-queens: the search, pruning and backtracking animation\" data-o=\"{}\"></div>\n<h2>Backtracking, brute force and DP</h2>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"dt fa\"><tr><th>Method</th><th>Idea</th><th>When it fits</th></tr><tr><td>Brute Force</td><td>Examines all states without pruning</td><td>Small space</td></tr><tr><td>Backtracking</td><td>Builds states incrementally and prunes invalid branches</td><td>Constraint-satisfaction problems (Sudoku, N-queens, graph colouring)</td></tr><tr><td>DP</td><td>Stores overlapping subproblems</td><td>When states repeat and the state is compact</td></tr></table></div>\n<p>Improvements: smart ordering of choices (most constrained variable), forward checking, branch and bound for optimization (upper/lower bounds for pruning).</p>", "obj": ["Model the state space as a tree and shrink the search with pruning.", "Implement the general backtracking template for combinatorial problems.", "State the difference between backtracking, brute force and DP."], "mis": ["Forgetting to \"undo the state\" (<code>cur.pop()</code>), which makes branches corrupt each other's state.", "Storing a reference to <code>cur</code> instead of a copy in the answer (<code>res.append(cur)</code> instead of <code>cur[:]</code>).", "\"Backtracking is always polynomial.\" ← It is exponential/factorial in the worst case; pruning only improves the constant."], "sum": ["Backtracking = DFS on the state-space tree + pruning invalid branches + undoing state.", "N-queens, permutations, subsets and Sudoku are common examples.", "Exponential in the worst case; pruning and smart ordering improve performance."], "ex": [{"q": "How many solutions does the 4-queens problem have?", "e": "For $N=4$ there are exactly 2 arrangements (check in the N-queens widget)."}, {"q": "Which step is the heart of the backtracking algorithm?", "e": "Un-choosing lets the next branch start from a clean state.", "o": ["Sorting the choices", "Undoing the change after the recursive call", "Using a queue", "Splitting into two halves"]}, {"q": "The number of permutations of $n$ distinct elements is $n!$ and generating all of them takes at least $\\Omega(n!)$ time.", "e": "The output alone has $n!$ permutations; generating them cannot be faster."}, {"q": "What pruning would you suggest for solving Sudoku with backtracking, and how does the order of filling cells matter?", "a": "Before placing a digit, check it is not repeated in the row, column and 3×3 box (basic pruning). Improvement: keep for every empty cell the set of possible values and fill first the cell with the fewest options (minimum remaining values), because it discovers contradictions sooner and prunes a larger branch. Forward checking shrinks the domains of neighbouring cells after each choice and backtracks if some domain becomes empty."}]});
addEN("a5-4",{"body": "\n<h2>The minimum spanning tree (MST)</h2>\n<p>For a connected, weighted, undirected graph, a <b>spanning tree</b> is a subset of edges that connects all vertices without a cycle ($V-1$ edges). An <b>MST</b> is the spanning tree with the least total weight. Uses: network design (cabling/piping at minimum cost), hierarchical clustering. Two key theorems: the <b>cut property</b>: the lightest edge crossing a cut belongs to some MST; and the <b>cycle property</b>: the heaviest edge of any cycle (with distinct weights) belongs to no MST.</p>\n<h3>Kruskal</h3>\n<p>Sort the edges ascending; accept each edge if its two endpoints are in different components (it creates no cycle). Cycle detection with <b>union-find</b>. Time: $O(E\\log E)$ (sorting dominates).</p>\n<div class=\"wbox\" data-w=\"kruskal\" data-t=\"Kruskal on the sample graph\" data-o=\"{}\"></div>\n<h3>Prim</h3>\n<p>Starts from one vertex and each time adds the lightest edge that connects the current tree to a new vertex (like Dijkstra with a priority queue): $O(E\\log V)$. For dense graphs Prim with a matrix/heap and for sparse graphs Kruskal is usually simpler.</p>\n<h2>Union-Find (Disjoint Set Union)</h2>\n<p>Two operations: <code>find(x)</code> the representative of the set of $x$ and <code>union(x,y)</code> merging two sets. With two optimizations, <b>path compression</b> and <b>union by rank/size</b>, the amortized cost of each operation is $O(\\alpha(n))$ where $\\alpha$ is the inverse Ackermann function and is below 5 for any practical $n$ — constant in practice.</p>\n<pre data-l=\"py\">\nclass DSU:\n    def __init__(self, n): self.p = list(range(n)); self.sz = [1]*n\n    def find(self, x):\n        while self.p[x] != x:\n            self.p[x] = self.p[self.p[x]]     # Path Halving\n            x = self.p[x]\n        return x\n    def union(self, a, b):\n        a, b = self.find(a), self.find(b)\n        if a == b: return False               # cycle\n        if self.sz[a] < self.sz[b]: a, b = b, a\n        self.p[b] = a; self.sz[a] += self.sz[b]\n        return True\n</pre>\n<h2>Topological sort (a reminder)</h2>\n<p>In a DAG, a linear order of the vertices in which every edge $u\\to v$ puts $u$ before $v$. Two methods: DFS (reverse post-order) or <b>Kahn's algorithm</b> (repeatedly remove vertices with in-degree zero using a queue; if not all vertices get removed, a cycle exists). Time $O(V+E)$. Uses: build systems, course scheduling, the execution order of a computation graph (autograd).</p>", "obj": ["Define the minimum spanning tree (MST) and compare Kruskal and Prim.", "Explain the union-find structure with path compression and union by rank.", "Apply topological sort to a DAG."], "mis": ["\"An MST gives the shortest path between vertices.\" ← An MST minimizes the total weight, not the distance between two particular vertices.", "\"An MST is always unique.\" ← Only if the weights are distinct."], "sum": ["An MST is built with Kruskal ($O(E\\log E)$) or Prim ($O(E\\log V)$), and both are greedy.", "Union-find with path compression and union by size is essentially amortized $O(1)$.", "Topological sort of a DAG is done with DFS or Kahn in $O(V+E)$."], "ex": [{"q": "How many edges does an MST of a connected graph with 10 vertices have?"}, {"q": "What structure does Kruskal use to detect that an edge would create a cycle?", "e": "If the two endpoints of an edge have the same representative, adding it would create a cycle.", "o": ["Stack", "Union-Find", "Trie", "Heap"]}, {"q": "Kahn's algorithm for topological sort can detect the presence of a cycle.", "e": "If after the queue ends some vertex still has a positive in-degree, there is a cycle."}, {"q": "Why is Kruskal with \"sorting by weight\" optimal? (cut property)", "a": "When we examine the smallest edge $e$ in the sorted list and its endpoints are in different components, $e$ is the lightest edge crossing a cut between the component of one endpoint and the rest. By the cut property some MST contains $e$, so adding it is safe. If both endpoints are in the same component, $e$ lies on a cycle all of whose other edges are lighter or equal, and rejecting it is safe."}]});
addEN("a6-1",{"body": "\n<h2>A selection framework</h2>\n<p>There is no absolute \"best\" data structure. Ask in order: (1) What is the <b>dominant operation</b> (reads/writes, point/range)? (2) The <b>data size</b> and growth rate? (3) Is <b>order</b> or <b>priority</b> needed? (4) The <b>memory</b> limit and the <b>cache</b> pattern? (5) <b>Concurrency</b> and durability?</p>\n<div class=\"wbox\" data-w=\"chooser\" data-t=\"A guide to choosing a data structure\" data-o=\"{}\"></div>\n<h2>Common trade-offs</h2>\n<ul><li><b>Memory versus time:</b> hash tables and indexes eat memory to make lookup fast; so do memo tables, caches and lookup tables. <b>Probabilistic</b> structures like the <i>Bloom filter</i> answer \"definitely not / probably yes\" with very little memory (used in LSM trees, caches).</li><li><b>Reads versus writes:</b> a sorted array makes reads cheap and writes expensive; an LSM tree makes writes cheap through sequential appends and leaves reads to background merges; a B+Tree balances both.</li><li><b>Simplicity versus performance:</b> sometimes a plain array plus one sort is faster and less error-prone than a complex structure.</li></ul>\n<h2>Optimizing algorithms</h2>\n<ol><li>First <b>correctness</b>, then <b>measurement</b> (profiling), and optimize only the real bottleneck.</li><li>Change the <b>algorithm and data structure</b> before micro-optimizing (going from $O(n^2)\\to O(n\\log n)$ matters more than any constant trick).</li><li>Remove repeated computation (memoization, prefix-sum preprocessing, two pointers).</li><li>Think about <b>locality</b>: contiguous arrays, SoA instead of AoS, batching I/O.</li></ol>\n<div class=\"wbox\" data-w=\"bench\" data-t=\"A real benchmark: search in an array, binary search and a set\" data-o=\"{}\"></div>\n<h2>Real-world uses</h2>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table class=\"dt fa\"><tr><th>Area</th><th>Data structure</th><th>Reason</th></tr><tr><td>Database index</td><td>B+Tree</td><td>A shallow, wide tree; minimal disk access, range scans through leaf links</td></tr><tr><td>Write-heavy key-value stores (RocksDB, Cassandra)</td><td>LSM-Tree + Bloom Filter</td><td>Fast sequential writes; a filter prevents useless reads</td></tr><tr><td>LRU cache</td><td>Hash Map + Doubly Linked List</td><td>$O(1)$ access and move</td></tr><tr><td>Task scheduling, Dijkstra</td><td>Heap</td><td>Extract-min in $O(\\log n)$</td></tr><tr><td>Text search</td><td>Inverted Index (Hash/Trie)</td><td>A map from term to a list of documents</td></tr><tr><td>Nearest-neighbour search (embeddings)</td><td>KD-tree, HNSW (graph), IVF</td><td>Vector index; approximate search for high dimensions</td></tr><tr><td>Sparse data in ML (one-hot features)</td><td>Sparse Matrix (CSR/COO)</td><td>Only the non-zero values are stored</td></tr><tr><td>Tensors in deep learning</td><td>Contiguous array + strides</td><td>Fast matrix multiplication on GPUs and cache-friendly</td></tr></table></div>\n<h3>The effect of data structures on databases and ML</h3>\n<p>In databases, choosing the index type (B+Tree for ranges, hash for equality) brings the query cost from an $O(n)$ scan to $O(\\log n)$, and a hash join ($O(n+m)$) versus a sort-merge join ($O(n\\log n)$) makes a huge difference. In ML, tensor memory layout (row-major, contiguous), batching and using sparse matrices change performance by tens of times; and in embedding retrieval, the vector-index structure trades off accuracy, latency and memory.</p>", "obj": ["Choose a suitable data structure based on the access pattern.", "Know the memory-versus-speed trade-off and the effect of the cache.", "Explain the effect of data structures on database and machine-learning workloads."], "mis": ["\"Always pick the hash table because it is $O(1)$.\" ← It is unsuitable if you need order/ranges or little memory.", "\"Premature optimization is better.\" ← Profile first, then optimize the bottleneck.", "\"Asymptotic complexity is the only criterion.\" ← Constants, cache and I/O are decisive."], "sum": ["Choose a data structure by the dominant operation, data size, order, memory and concurrency.", "Memory/time and read/write trade-offs are everywhere: hash, B+Tree, LSM, Bloom filter.", "Algorithm and data structure first, then constants; always with profiling.", "Data structures in DB and ML (index, join, tensor, sparse matrix) directly determine cost."], "ex": [{"q": "What combination is needed to implement an LRU cache with $O(1)$ get and put?", "e": "A hash table makes node access $O(1)$ and a doubly linked list makes moving to the front and evicting the tail $O(1)$.", "o": ["A sorted array", "Hash Map + Doubly Linked List", "BST", "Stack"]}, {"q": "For a very high write volume with point reads, which storage structure is usually more suitable?", "e": "An LSM tree gathers writes in memory and writes them sequentially to disk; a Bloom filter makes reads efficient.", "o": ["B+Tree", "LSM-Tree", "A sorted array", "Trie"]}, {"q": "A Bloom filter may give a \"false positive\" but not a \"false negative\".", "e": "If it says \"not present\" it is definitely not present; if it says \"present\" it may be wrong."}, {"q": "For a service with 10 million records where 90% of requests are \"lookup by id\" and 10% are \"list records between two dates\", what structure(s) do you suggest?", "a": "For lookup by id: a hash index or a B+Tree on the id (hash is more efficient for equality). For the date range: a B+Tree on the date column (range scan via leaf links), or, if data is inserted in time order, a clustered index/partitioning on the date. Altogether two indexes (cost: memory and slower writes) and measurement on the real workload. If writes are heavy, an LSM is also an option.\n"}]});
